{ Opening moves should do visible work: influence the center, develop pieces to useful squares, and prepare king safety. Principles help you generate candidates; threats decide the move. } 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 { Illustrative line. Nf3 develops with a target. Black can answer ...Nc6, defending e5 while bringing out a piece. Bc4 then develops the bishop and looks toward f7. These are useful moves because their jobs can be explained from the board. They are not a script to follow regardless of the opponent. If Black creates a concrete threat or changes the center, stop and inspect that change before making your next familiar move. } *

