[SetUp "1"]

{ Exercise. Try the questions in the book before reading this line. } 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 { Illustrative line. Nc3 attacks d5 and e4. The d5-pawn is the central point of contact with White's c4-pawn. The knight also develops toward the center. ...Nf6 develops a knight and protects d5. It helps Black maintain the central pawn rather than reacting to development with an unrelated move. Nf3 develops the other knight, controls d4 and e5, and clears g1 for eventual castling. White still needs to move the f1-bishop and verify king safety before castling. Several sound opening continuations exist. } *

