{ Exercise. Try the questions in the book before reading this line. } 1. Be2 Bxe2 2. Kxe2 { Illustrative line. The bishop attacks f1, the square the king would cross. The path between a6 and f1 is clear in this diagram. No. White cannot castle through the attacked square f1. Castling rights record move history; they do not guarantee that castling is legal now. Be2 blocks the diagonal. If Black exchanges with ...Bxe2, Kxe2 removes that bishop. Moving the king permanently gives up castling rights. This illustrates a legal way to resolve the diagonal, not a uniquely best conversion of White's large material advantage. } *

